From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ryan Murray <81541646+RyanTheTide@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 22:19:01 +1100
Subject: [PATCH] thunderbolt: Unregister XDomains when the domain is
 removed

Since XDomains are removed from the bus without holding tb->lock,
tb_xdomain_remove() only takes an XDomain out of the topology; the
caller unregisters it later with tb_domain_unregister_unplugged_xdomains().
tb_domain_remove() never does that. Stopping the connection manager
removes the root switch, which marks every XDomain unplugged and calls
tb_xdomain_remove(), but the XDomain stays registered on the bus and its
service drivers stay bound.

When the domain is then freed, a bound thunderbolt-net keeps its
protocol handler on the global list with xd->tb pointing at the freed
domain. A host router driver that removes and re-adds its domain when
the link restarts (the Apple ACIO driver does so when the partner
reboots) then gets a new domain with the same index: adding the new
XDomain fails with a duplicate "0-1" sysfs name, and the stale
thunderbolt-net answers the partner's ThunderboltIP login, which now
arrives on the new control channel, through the freed one:

  Unable to handle kernel paging request at virtual address ...
  pc : queued_spin_lock_slowpath+0x18c/0x5d0
  Call trace:
   queued_spin_lock_slowpath+0x18c/0x5d0 (P)
   _raw_spin_lock_irqsave+0x9c/0xa8
   __mutex_unlock_slowpath+0x90/0x338
   mutex_unlock+0x34/0x70
   tb_cfg_request+0x218/0x250 [thunderbolt]
   tb_xdomain_response+0x74/0xb0 [thunderbolt]
   tbnet_login_response+0x9c/0x238 [thunderbolt_net]
   tbnet_handle_packet+0xd0/0x358 [thunderbolt_net]
   tb_xdomain_handle_request+0xf0/0x1f0 [thunderbolt]
   tb_domain_event_cb+0x60/0xa8 [thunderbolt]
   tb_ctl_handle_event+0x38/0x170 [thunderbolt]
   tb_ctl_rx_callback+0x1b8/0x528 [thunderbolt]
   ring_work+0x138/0x280 [thunderbolt]

Unregister the unplugged XDomains in tb_domain_remove() once tb->lock
is dropped and the domain workqueue is drained, before the connection
manager is deinitialized and the domain device goes away. The control
channel is already stopped at that point, so requests the service
drivers send while unbinding (thunderbolt-net's logout) fail with
-ENOTCONN instead of waiting for a timeout.

Fixes: a8937f35cf39 ("thunderbolt: Remove XDomain from the bus without holding tb->lock")
Closes: https://github.com/aurora-silicon/linux/issues/1318
Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Ryan Murray <81541646+RyanTheTide@users.noreply.github.com>
(cherry picked from commit 09458b01af1c31919327cad070f17810ade588c3)
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 drivers/thunderbolt/domain.c | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/thunderbolt/domain.c b/drivers/thunderbolt/domain.c
index 56ffea80c5..72b227997f 100644
--- a/drivers/thunderbolt/domain.c
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/domain.c
@@ -510,6 +510,17 @@ void tb_domain_remove(struct tb *tb)
 
 	flush_workqueue(tb->wq);
 
+	/*
+	 * Stopping the connection manager removed the XDomains from the
+	 * topology and marked them unplugged, but they are still on the bus
+	 * with their service drivers bound. Unregister them now: a service
+	 * driver keeps its protocol handler registered until it is unbound
+	 * and would otherwise keep answering XDomain requests through
+	 * tb->ctl after the domain is gone. The control channel is stopped,
+	 * so whatever the service drivers send while unbinding fails at once.
+	 */
+	tb_domain_unregister_unplugged_xdomains(tb);
+
 	if (tb->cm_ops->deinit)
 		tb->cm_ops->deinit(tb);
 
